Australian Product Guide — Brand Fidelity v32.5.2 certified presentation release
Release date: 2026-08-18
Status: CURRENT RELEASE CANDIDATE / EXACT PRODUCTION CERTIFICATION REQUIRED AFTER MERGE
Production source: GitHub main via Vercel
Canonical production domain: https://australianproductguide.au

Current platform: the underlying APG application/data capability remains the maintained priority-commerce / Decision Intelligence v4.1 / Evidence & Commerce Depth v27 / Trust Infrastructure v28 / Amazon Conversion v29 stack, with Premium Brand v30, Premium Theme v31.1 and Brand Fidelity v32.5.2 forming the controlling presentation system once this release is merged and exact-Production certified. The architecture remains lightweight SSR-first Node with progressive enhancement. v32.5.2 changes presentation and release controls only; it does not change product-fit logic, catalogue data, retailer ranking, affiliate weighting, account/auth behaviour, analytics consent or privacy controls.

Brand-fidelity truth: the owner-approved APG brand board is implemented as a coherent production identity rather than an approximate A-shaped logo. The current mark uses the traced four-part APG geometry and first-party responsive assets for primary horizontal, compact, APG monogram, standalone symbol, white, monochrome, favicon, app-icon and social applications. Desktop uses the full Australian Product Guide lock-up. Standard tablet/mobile widths use the APG symbol/monogram relationship, with the monogram P kept white on navy so the complete APG mark remains legible.

Colour-system truth: the controlling palette is APG Blue #2563EB, APG Navy #0F172A, APG Teal #06B6D4, APG Green #10B981, Light Grey #F1F5F9 and Slate #64748B, supported by white and accessible light/dark text tokens. Navy is used for institutional authority and deep surfaces; blue is the primary action colour; teal/green remain selective accents; white/light grey provide breathing space. Dark surfaces use white or sufficiently light foreground text rather than low-contrast navy/grey combinations.

Maintained-research highlight: the successful historical yellow treatment was recovered from APG source history rather than guessed. The MAINTAINED AUSTRALIAN RESEARCH band uses #FFD65B → #F4BB45 → #F2B348, with #E0A630/#D89C24 border tones and dark foreground treatment. The main proof text remains based on the recovered #082735 family and the independence copy is explicitly #2B4B56 at strong weight. Yellow remains a strategic trust accent rather than a primary site colour. Product/category/brand values remain data-driven from the maintained catalogue rather than hard-coded release numbers.

Scout and proof clearance: Scout remains APG's decision-assistance product and uses the APG Blue + white master symbol consistently after JavaScript initialisation. v32.2 prevents the legacy mascot overwriting the approved APG mark. v32.3 makes the fixed desktop launcher a compact 58px APG-symbol control so it cannot obscure the yellow research independence statement. Scout decision logic, structured-session continuity and zero-commercial-weight recommendation controls are unchanged.

Mobile navigation history and final semantic fix: manual Production screenshot review repeatedly overruled green technical assertions where the rendered UI disagreed. v32.4 first reconciled the intended Decision Lab / Ask Scout hierarchy. v32.5 moved the controls into deterministic SSR/CSS presentation, and v32.5.1 removed a self-triggering MutationObserver discovered by the focused Production gate. The exact v32.5.1 tablet/mobile screenshot then exposed one residual small Decision Lab link above the approved primary Decision Lab and Ask Scout controls. That legacy item was not a `.mobile-power` element, so the earlier focused gate incorrectly reported zero duplicate power actions. v32.5.2 corrects the acceptance boundary from CSS class to semantic destination: within the mobile-nav root there may be exactly one `/decision-lab/` destination in total and it must be the approved primary control. Server rendering removes any additional Decision Lab anchor regardless of legacy class, CSS hides any later direct duplicate, and bounded client reconciliation removes any later semantic duplicate without using MutationObserver. The intended visible hierarchy is therefore exactly Decision Lab → Ask Scout → Popular products → Research & compare → Trust & transparency.

Catalogue and commerce truth: catalogue, retailer, Amazon and recommendation facts are verified from the live observability contract during exact-Production certification rather than copied into the interface as release constants. Affiliate participation, retailer availability and commission contribute zero recommendation points.

Imagery truth: the governed registry continues to distinguish authorised external exact-model photography from first-party/category-correct authored decision visuals. APG does not scrape Amazon product pages, reverse-engineer image URLs, fabricate real-product photography or claim third-party imagery rights it does not hold. Authorised genuine-product image delivery remains a separate ingestion task where rights/delivery are not available.

Responsive and accessibility truth: the full v32 Production matrix covers homepage, search, category, product, comparison, Decision Lab, My APG, trust/footer and Scout across desktop, tablet and mobile. v32.5.2 retains the additional exact-Production tablet/mobile visual gate but strengthens it to count every top-level semantic `/decision-lab/` anchor, requiring exactly one total and one visible destination, requiring that destination to be the approved primary control, verifying the CSS-rendered Decision Lab and Ask Scout labels and actual vertical order ahead of the accordion groups, rejecting visible unclassified power actions, checking page width, and saving dedicated menu screenshots for manual review.

Implementation history: PR #79 established v32.2 board-faithful identity and Scout/mobile corrections. PR #81 introduced v32.3 after screenshot review found residual yellow-proof contrast and Scout-clearance defects. v32.4 introduced the first mobile hierarchy reconciliation. v32.5 made it SSR/CSS-stable, v32.5.1 removed the observer-loop runtime defect, and the manually reviewed v32.5.1 screenshot then exposed the remaining non-power semantic Decision Lab duplicate. v32.5.2 is the final semantic-destination correction and is not complete until its exact Production screenshot itself visually shows one Decision Lab followed by one Ask Scout with no extra Decision Lab link.

Release verification requirement: this file is included in the same v32.5.2 implementation PR so the final merged GitHub main SHA can remain the exact SHA certified. Completion requires Vercel READY on the canonical domain, APG v27 Visual Certification success, APG v32 Brand Visual Certification success and APG v32.5.2 Mobile Visual Certification success on the same SHA, publication of the final screenshot artefacts, manual inspection of the v32.5.2 tablet/mobile screenshots, and a clear post-release Vercel error/fatal log window.

Remaining external gaps: lawful genuine-product photography ingestion remains incomplete until an authorised delivery/right basis exists. Hosted Supabase Auth settings outside the connected writable surface, including any previously identified leaked-password-protection setting unless subsequently verified, remain separate owner/admin configuration work. Formal trade mark clearance/registration is not claimed by this release.
